<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Atelier creation models may run on LM Studio or Ollama at a
         user-selected local-network address. Keep this permission in the
         signed app so URLSession is allowed to open that outbound socket. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>

    <key>com.apple.security.virtualization</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- The app runs with the hardened runtime, which turns on library
         validation: a loaded dylib must carry the same Team ID as the
         process. libjcross_engine_glm.dylib is built by Cargo and embedded
         ad hoc, so it has no Team ID and can never match. dyld then refuses
         it and the app aborts at launch before any UI appears.

         The failure is worth naming because it reads as something else: the
         first line dyld prints is "Library not loaded", which points at a
         missing file, while the real cause sits further down in the list of
         paths it tried.

         The alternative fix is an ad hoc deep re-sign of the bundle, which
         also makes it launch. It works by dropping the hardened runtime
         altogether, giving up notarisation and every other protection the
         runtime carries in order to load one dylib. This entitlement gives
         up library validation alone.

         Note for editors: XML forbids a double hyphen inside a comment, and
         codesign passes this file to AMFI, whose parser enforces that. A
         comment mentioning a command line flag verbatim breaks signing with
         "AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error", pointing at the comment's line
         rather than at anything wrong with the entitlements themselves. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
